 stack (numpy/scipy/pandas absent; network disabled; GPU disabled). Therefore zero CycleNet setup facts are verifiable from local sources. Any concrete CycleNet parameters in the design must be sourced from the public CycleNet (NeurIPS 2024, ACAT-SCUT/CycleNet) paper/repo and treated as To-Confirm items. The design is self-contained and execution-ready (to be run where assets exist), not dependent on this sandbox.\\n\\n**2. Proposed high-level approach (sketch).**\\n\\n*Stage A \\u2014 autocorrelation-based period detection (input side).*\\n- For each dataset, use only the standardized training split.\\n- Compute the autocorrelation function (ACF) at lags 1..L (L ~ a few thousand steps), normalized to [-1, 1]; optionally detrend or first-difference first to suppress spurious peaks.\\n- Candidate cycle lengths = lags that are ACF local maxima above a threshold (e.g., ACF > 2/sqrt(N); or a fixed floor like > 0.3) with a minimum lag separation; rank candidates by ACF magnitude \\u2192 top-k (k\\u22485) candidates.\\n- Cross-check with an FFT periodogram (dominant frequency) to validate the top ACF period.\\n- Record the full ACF curve so it can be overlaid on the accuracy curve.\\n\\n*Stage B \\u2014 CycleNet cycle-length sweep (model side).*\\n- Fix a single backbone (e.g., DLinear or PatchTST, per CycleNet's official design) and fix all hyperparameters (seq_len, hidden dims, time-index embedding dim, lr, epochs, batch size, train/val/test split 7:1:2).\\n- For each dataset \\u00d7 prediction horizon (e.g., pred_len \\u2208 {96, 192, 336, 720}), sweep cycle_len over: (i) the ACF top-k candidate periods, (ii) positive control lengths = known true periods of the data (e.g., 24h/168h for hourly; 144 for 10-min; 288 for 5-min), (iii) negative/off-peak control lengths (e.g., \\u00b110\\u201350% around the true period and a few arbitrary non-peak values).\\n- Run each configuration with \\u22653 seeds; optimize a validation loss (not test) to select settings; report test MSE (primary) and MAE (secondary).\\n\\n*Stage C \\u2014 linking autocorrelation to \\\"optimal\\\" cycle length.*\\n- For each dataset/horizon, plot test MSE (and val MSE) vs cycle_len, marking ACF candidate periods on the same axis.\\n- Quantify the link: (a) Does a length within the ACF top-k set achieve the best test MSE? (b) \\\"Cycle-length gap\\\" = |ACF-selected best period \\u2212 empirically best cycle_len|; (c) surface sharpness (ratio of MSE at ACF-selected length vs at optimal length) to judge sensitivity.\\n\\n**3. Consequential assumptions (to agree before final design).**\\n- \\\"Optimal cycle length\\\" = the cycle_len minimizing predictive error (test MSE) on a fixed backbone, all else held constant \\u2014 to be compared against the ACF-peak-defined length, not assumed equal.\\n- CycleNet configuration: RCM with a single integer cycle_len (period in time steps) + cycle-aware time-index encoding; official repo defaults (ACAT-SCUT/CycleNet).\\n- Autocorrelation computed on the (standardized, possibly detrended) training split; peak-detection heuristics as above.\\n- Single-cycle assumption: CycleNet's RCM models one dominant cycle; multi-periodic series (daily+weekly in Electricity/Traffic) are handled by choosing the primary period.\\n- Optimality is horizon-dependent (cycle component matters more at long horizons); so \\\"optimal cycle length\\\" should be reported per pred_len, not as one global number.\\n- Fixed seed/trial scheme needed to make the MSE surface trustworthy.\\n\\n**4. Key uncertainties.**\\n- Exact public defaults per dataset (cycle_len, time_enc_dim, backbones) \\u2014 must be confirmed against the official CycleNet repo/scripts.\\n- Whether \\\"optimal\\\" should be defined on validation (to avoid test-set selection) vs. test \\u2014 need an agreed selection rule.\\n- Whether ACF-dominant period (max peak) coincides with predictive-optimal cycle_len; plausibly ACF identifies the data's natural period, but CycleNet's optimal length