{"id": "d96082c0-93a8-4a96-bd63-4f30d4557c3a", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:18:09.448574", "source": "user", "parent_id": "7ad78c95-781e-41ff-a2d4-1a383071b709", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approved. The proposed three-stage approach (ACF-based period detection on the training split, a controlled cycle_len sweep on a fixed CycleNet backbone with positive/negative controls and ≥3 seeds, then linkage metrics per pred_len) is sound and covers rationale, controls, analysis plan, and feasibility. We agree on the following before the main work: (1) \\\"optimal cycle length\\\" = the cycle_len minimizing predictive error on a fixed backbone, compared against (not assumed equal to) the ACF-peak period; (2) model settings are selected on validation loss and test MSE is reported as confirmatory (per pred_len), avoiding test-set snooping; (3) CycleNet follows the official ACAT-SCUT/CycleNet single-integer cycle_len + RCM design, with exact per-dataset defaults (cycle_len, time_enc_dim, backbone) marked To-Confirm from the public repo/paper since no local source exists; (4) autocorrelation uses standardized/detrended training data, ACF local-maxima candidates above threshold with minimum lag separation, cross-checked by FFT; single dominant cycle assumed for multi-periodic series; (5) only cycle_len varies across sweeps — all other hyperparameters, splits (7:1:2), and seeds fixed. Proceed to produce the final experiment design deliverable: the formal experiment with clear rationale, controls (positive = known true periods, negative = off-peak/non-peak lengths, plus a fixed-hyperparameter baseline), a concrete analysis plan (per-dataset/per-horizon MSE-vs-cycle_len curves with ACF candidates overlaid, cycle-length gap, surface sharpness/sensitivity), and a feasibility statement that the design is portable and execution-ready in a provisioned environment (CPU-only caveat noted) with the ACF/optimality definitions made explicit. State explicitly that no experiment was run. Do not fabricate any results or dataset numbers beyond what is sourced/To-Confirm.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "958de4eb-d34b-45df-ba20-199d211d7199", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:18:31.185444", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "d96082c0-93a8-4a96-bd63-4f30d4557c3a", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "The plan is approved. I'll now produce the complete experiment design deliverable (stage=result), with definitions made explicit, a formal procedure, controls, analysis plan, and feasibility, while clearly stating that no experiment was run and marking all dataset/model numbers as To-Confirm.\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher approved the plan and wants the final design deliverable. I need to produce a comprehensive, formal experiment design (stage=result) with:\n- clear rationale\n- controls (positive, negative, baseline)\n- concrete analysis plan\n- feasibility statement\n- explicit definitions of ACF/optimality\n- state no experiment was run\n- do not fabricate results\n\nLet me structure the final design carefully. I should provide a thorough write-up. Let me organize:\n\n1. Title / Question\n2. Definitions (explicit)\n   - \"Optimal cycle length\" (predictive-accuracy-optimal on validation)\n   - Autocorrelation (ACF definition), peak detection\n   - CycleNet cycle_len meaning\n3. Rationale (why this question matters, why approach works)\n4. Materials: datasets (with periods — but must mark exact ones To-Confirm from official repo; present the known periodicities as assumptions/source-supported where possible but flag), CycleNet setup\n5. Independent/dependent variables, fixed settings\n6. Experimental design\n   - Stage A: ACF period detection\n   - Stage B: cycle_len sweep\n   - Stage C: linkage metrics\n7. Controls\n8. Analysis plan (metrics, plots, tables, hypothesis tests if any)\n9. Feasibility\n10. Assumptions & explicit statement no experiment run\n11. Reporting plan\n\nLet me be careful about numbers: I should not fabricate dataset-specific numbers. I can present the general design and mark placeholders for exact numbers as To-Confirm. Known facts I can mention as \"public knowledge, To-Confirm\": CycleNet paper uses ETT, Electricity, Traffic, Weather, PEMS, Solar; cycle length examples 24 (hourly daily), 144 (10-min daily), 288 (5-min daily), 168 (hourly weekly). These are To-Confirm but reasonable to state as such.\n\nLet me define ACF formula precisely:\nACF at lag h: r(h) = Σ_t (x_t - x̄)(x_{t+h} - x̄) / Σ_t (x_t - x̄)^2, for h in 1..L.\n\nPeak detection: local maxima: r(h) > r(h-1) and r(h) > r(h+1) (and typically r(h) ≥ r(h-j) within a window of a few lags); threshold r(h) > c where c = max(0.3, 2/sqrt(N)) or a permutation-based critical value; min separation between consecutive candidate peaks ≥ Δ (e.g., 5 lags or 2% of L). Rank by r(h) value; top-k.\n\nFFT cross-check: periodogram argmax frequency → period estimate f = N / k where k = argmax magnitude; compare.\n\nCycle-length gap: gap = |c*_ACF − c*_opt| where c*_opt is the empirically best cycle_len on validation.\n\nSurface sharpness: ratio MSE(c*_ACF)/MSE(c*_opt) or normalized: (MSE(c)-MSE_min)/MSE_min; report at c*_ACF.\n\nAgreement: does top-1 ACF candidate achieve within X% (e.g., 1% or within 1 std of seed noise) of the best validation MSE?\n\nPer-dataset tables.\n\nStatistics: mean±std over seeds; paired comparison? Could use a Wilcoxon or just report means. Keep it simple—descriptive plus a \"coverage\" metric: fraction of datasets where ACF top-k contains the empirical optimum (this is like a retrieval/recall@k metric). That's a nice concrete analysis plan.\n\nLet me also define \"optimality is horizon-dependent\": report optimal cycle_len per (dataset, pred_len).\n\nSweep design: cycle lengths to include, e.g. for hourly data: {1, 3, 6, 12, 24, 36, 48, 72, 96, 120, 144, 168, 192, 240, 336, 336+...}. But should adapt per dataset based on ACF candidates + known periods. Provide a generic rule: candidates = ACF top-5 ∪ {known true periods} ∪ {controls: integer multiples/harmonics around true period, e.g., true×{0.5,0.75,1.25,1.5,2}, and a few off-peak lengths (e.g., true±10%, true±25%)} ∪ {default 24/144/288 baseline}. For sub-hourly, scale accordingly.\n\nSweep count feasibility: per dataset ~ 10-15 cycle_len values × 4 horizons × 3 seeds × multiple datasets. Each CycleNet training run with DLinear is quick on GPU; on CPU it's slower but fine for seq_len 336, pred_len up to 720. Note CPU-only caveat.\n\nSelection rule: pick cycle_len that minimizes mean validation MSE over seeds; report test MSE at that choice.\n\nLet me write the final deliverable. It should be comprehensive but organized. I'll put the full design in the `answer` field, evidence in `evidence`, assumptions, uncertainties.\n\nOne more consideration: the researcher said \"Do not fabricate any results or dataset numbers beyond what is sourced/To-Confirm.\" So I must avoid inventing numbers. I'll clearly mark which values are To-Confirm placeholders.\n\nLet me also include explicit feasibility: the design is portable; in this sandbox no packages, no network, no GPU, no data → cannot execute; that's fine for design-only. Execution-ready in provisioned environment. CPU-only caveat: reduce budget if needed.\n\nI'll produce the result handoff now.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design: Using Autocorrelation Analysis to Identify the Optimal Cycle Length for CycleNet in Time Series Forecasting\n\n> **Status: design-only. No experiment was run, and no results are reported or fabricated.** All numbers not anchored to the official CycleNet paper/repo are marked **To-Confirm** and are placeholders to be resolved against the public source before execution.\n\n## 1. Explicit definitions\n\n**D1 — Autocorrelation (ACF).** For a training-series segment x of length T, detrended/standardized to mean 0, define the sample autocorrelation at lag h (1≤h≤L): r(h) = Σ_{t=1}^{T−h} x_t·x_{t+h} / Σ_{t=1}^{T} x_t². Candidate periods are lags h where r(h) is a **local maximum** (r(h)>r(h−1) and r(h)>r(h+1)), **above a threshold** c = max(0.3, 2/√T), with a **minimum separation** Δ between consecutive accepted peaks (Δ≈5 lags). Candidates are ranked by r(h); the **top-k** (k≈5) lags are the ACF-selected candidate cycle lengths. **FFT periodogram** (argmax magnitude → period f = T/argmaxbin) is used as an independent cross-check of the top ACF period.\n\n**D2 — \"Optimal cycle length\".** For a fixed backbone and fixed hyperparameters, the optimal cycle_len is the value minimizing **mean validation MSE** across seeds; reported **per (dataset, pred_len)** because optimality is assumed horizon-dependent. Test MSE is reported only as a confirmatory check on the validation-selected choice (test set never used for selection — no data snooping).\n\n**D3 — CycleNet cycle_len.** A single positive integer (period in time steps) controlling the Residual Cycle Forecasting Module (RCM), combined with a cycle-aware time-index embedding, per the official ACAT-SCUT/CycleNet design. Changing cycle_len re-parameterizes the time-index to the given periodicity; it does not change the backbone.\n\n## 2. Rationale\n\nCycleNet requires an externally supplied cycle length; the official workflow hard-codes period values per dataset. Autocorrelation is a natural, data-driven estimator of the dominant period. The research question is whether the ACF-dominant period coincides with — and can therefore predict — the cycle_len that minimizes predictive error. The experiment operationalizes this as: compute ACF candidates on the training split → sweep CycleNet over ACF candidates plus controls → measure how well ACF-predicted lengths recover the empirically optimal length and how much accuracy is lost if an off-peak length is chosen.\n\n## 3. Materials (To-Confirm against the official repo/paper)\n\n- **CycleNet implementation:** official ACAT-SCUT/CycleNet (RCM + cycle-aware time indices). Backbone to fix: **DLinear** as primary; **PatchTST** as a robustness extension (To-Confirm available in the official repo). Default backbone and per-dataset defaults (cycle_len, time_enc_dim, seq_len, pred_len, lr, epochs, seed) are read from the official run scripts and recorded in a setup table before execution. Any value not present in the official scripts is marked To-Confirm.\n- **Datasets (public knowledge, To-Confirm exact sampling rates/periods):** ETT (h1/h2 hourly, m1/m2 15-min), Electricity (hourly), Traffic (hourly), Weather (10-min per original paper — confirm), PEMS-series (5-min), Solar-Energy (10-min), optionally METR-LA / PEMS-BAY (5-min). Known natural periods (ground truth): daily = 24 steps (hourly), 96 (15-min), 144 (10-min), 288 (5-min); weekly = 168 (hourly). Exact availability in the official data scripts is To-Confirm.\n- **Compute:** provisioned environment with GPU for full runs; CPU-only fallback budget (fewer horizons/epochs) documented in feasibility. This sandbox (no network, no installed numpy/scipy/pandas, no datasets, no GPU) cannot execute it.\n\n## 4. Experimental procedure\n\n**Stage A — ACF period detection (input side).**\n1. Load each dataset's training split (official 7:1:2 split).\n2. Standardize per-channel (z-score); optionally detrend (or first-difference) and report both variants; handle multi-channel series by averaging the ACF across channels or by per-channel detection with majority pooling (decide once, record).\n3. Compute ACF r(h) for h = 1..L, L ≈ min(T/3, 3000).\n4. Peak detection per D1 → ranked candidate list C_ACF (top-k ≈5), plus top FFT period.\n5. Record the full ACF curve for overlay.\n\n**Stage B — CycleNet cycle_len sweep (model side).**\n1. Fix all hyperparameters except cycle_len; fix train/val/test splits and seeds (≥3).\n2. For each dataset × pred_len (e.g., 96/192/336/720 — To-Confirm official set), sweep the cycle_len set S:\n   - positive controls: known true periods of that dataset (e.g., 24, 168 for hourly; scaled for sub-hourly);\n   - ACF candidates: C_ACF;\n   - negative/off-peak controls: non-peak lengths around the true period (e.g., true×{0.5, 0.75, 1.25, 1.5, 2.0} and true±10%, true±25%);\n   - default baseline: the official per-dataset default cycle_len.\n   Expected |S| ≈ 10–15 values per (dataset, pred_len).\n3. Train each config with ≥3 seeds; record mean±std validation MSE/MAE and test MSE/MAE (test untouched during selection).\n\n**Stage C — Linking ACF to optimal length.**\n1. Build per-(dataset, pred_len) curves: mean validation MSE vs cycle_len, with C_ACF candidates and true periods overlaid on the same axis.\n2. Choose c*_opt = argmin mean val MSE per (dataset, pred_len); confirm with test MSE.\n\n## 5. Controls\n\n- **Positive controls:** known true periods (24/168 hourly; scaled for 15-min/10-min/5-min) — check the sweep's optimum recovers ground truth.\n- **Negative/off-peak controls:** lengths deliberately away from any ACF peak and away from the true period — expected to yield worse (or not better) validation MSE; evidence the surface is meaningful.\n- **Baseline:** official per-dataset default cycle_len with identical hyperparameters — anchors results to the published protocol and quantifies the cost/benefit of ACF-based selection vs the hand-set default.\n- **Fixed-hyperparameter control:** every configuration shares the same seq_len/dims/lr/epochs/split/seed scheme so only cycle_len varies.\n- **Seed control:** ≥3 seeds per config; conclusions drawn only on means consistent across seeds.\n\n## 6. Analysis plan (metrics and outputs)\n\n- **Primary metric:** validation MSE (means across seeds); secondary: MAE; confirmatory: test MSE at the validation-selected length.\n- **Outputs:** (i) per-dataset ACF curve with detected peaks vs the sweep's MSE-vs-cycle_len curve on one figure; (ii) per-(dataset, pred_len) table: C_ACF, c*_opt, official default, and MSE at each.\n- **Linkage metric L1 (ACF-optimum agreement):** does c*_opt fall within the ACF top-k candidate set? Report **coverage@k** = fraction of (dataset, pred_len) cells where the empirical optimum is in the ACF top-k (retrieval-style evaluation across all cells).\n- **Linkage metric L2 (cycle-length gap):** gap = |c*_ACF − c*_opt| for the top ACF candidate c*_ACF (top-1), in steps and as % of c*_opt.\n- **Linkage metric L3 (surface sharpness/sensitivity):** normalized loss lift at the ACF-selected length, lift(c*_ACF) = (MSE(c*_ACF) − MSE(c*_opt)) / MSE(c*_opt); also the half-width of cycle_len values within 1% of the optimum (measures how tolerant selection is).\n- **Decision rule (primary claim):** autocorrelation identifies the optimal cycle length if coverage@1 ≥ 0.6 of cells (i.e., top-1 ACF period recovers the empirical optimum in ≥60% of (dataset, pred_len) cells) and median lift(c*_ACF) ≤ 1%; coverage@5 ≥ 0.8 as a secondary robustness claim. These thresholds are prespecified.\n- **Robustness:** repeat coverage on test MSE (confirmatory), with the detrended-ACF variant, with per-channel vs pooled ACF, and with PatchTST backbone for a subset of cells.\n\n## 7. Feasibility\n\n- The design is **portable and execution-ready**: it requires only the official CycleNet repo, the public datasets, and a GPU or CPU environment with a working torch stack — none present in this sandbox (no network, no deps, no data, no GPU), and this task is design-only.\n- **GPU path:** ~6–8 datasets × 4 pred_len × ~12 cycle_len × 3 seeds ≈ 900–1200 training runs; DLinear runs are minutes each on GPU → feasible.\n- **CPU-only fallback** (if ever executed here, which is not required): reduce to 2 datasets × 2 pred_len × ~8 cycle_len × 3 seeds and limit epochs; still valid for the linkage analysis though lower-powered. Budget noted as a caveat, not a design change.\n\n## 8. Assumptions, uncertainties, and provenance\n\n**Assumptions (fixed requirements are marked):** (A1) optimal = min validation MSE per (dataset, pred_len) (fixed requirement). (A2) ACF computed on standardized/detrended training split with threshold+separation peak detection (requirement). (A3) single dominant cycle per run for multi-periodic series (requirement). (A4) only cycle_len varies; all else fixed (requirement). Remaining assumptions are To-Confirm: exact official per-dataset cycle_len/time_enc_dim/backbone/hyperparameters; dataset sampling-rate map; official pred_len set; official split scheme.\n\n**Uncertainties to be resolved at execution:** ACF peak may not coincide with predictive optimum at long horizons (exactly what is measured); multi-periodic dominance (daily vs weekly) ambiguity; whether validation-based optimum differs materially from test optimum; effect of the detrending choice on peaks; channel-pooling choice for multivariate ACF.\n\n**Provenance:** the only source-supported material in this workspace is task.json stating this is a design-only task; all CycleNet/dataset specifics above are from public knowledge of the CycleNet (NeurIPS 2024, ACAT-SCUT/CycleNet) paper/repo and are explicitly To-Confirm. No experiment was run; no numeric results are claimed beyond the prespecified decision thresholds (coverage@1 ≥ 0.6, median lift ≤ 1%, coverage@5 ≥ 0.8), which are design choices, not outcomes.", "evidence": "Inspection evidence: /workspace contains only task.json; documents.track.md = 'Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.' No CycleNet code, no track.md file, no datasets. Environment: Python 3.14.4; numpy/scipy/pandas not installed (import errors); network disabled; GPU disabled (224 CPU cores); datasets=[]. Therefore the design is grounded in the public CycleNet paper/repo (RCM + cycle-aware time indices; DLinear/PatchTST backbones; ETT/Electricity/Traffic/Weather/PEMS/Solar benchmarks), all marked To-Confirm. Design-only; no experiment run; decision thresholds (coverage@1≥0.6, median lift≤1%, coverage@5≥0.8) are prespecified analytic criteria, not observed results.", "assumptions": ["Optimal cycle length = the cycle_len minimizing mean validation MSE per (dataset, pred_len); test MSE is confirmatory only.", "ACF computed on standardized/detrended training split; candidates = local maxima above max(0.3, 2/sqrt(T)) with minimum separation; top-k (k=5) ranked; FFT periodogram as cross-check.", "Single dominant cycle assumed per run for multi-periodic series (daily vs weekly ambiguity measured, not assumed away).", "Only cycle_len varies across sweeps; all other hyperparameters, 7:1:2 splits, and >=3 seeds fixed.", "CycleNet configured per official ACAT-SCUT/CycleNet single-integer cycle_len + RCM with cycle-aware time indices.", "Execution requires a provisioned environment with the official repo, datasets, and a working torch stack; absent in this sandbox (network disabled, no deps, no data, no GPU).", "Exact per-dataset defaults (cycle_len, time_enc_dim, backbone, sampling-rate map, official pred_len set) are To-Confirm from the official repo/paper and recorded in a setup table before execution."], "uncertainties": ["Exact official defaults per dataset and whether the chosen backbone (DLinear/PatchTST) reproduces the published schedule.", "Whether the ACF-dominant period coincides with the predictive optimum, especially at long horizons where the cyclical component matters more — this is the measured effect, not an assumed equality.", "Multi-periodic dominance: hourly data with both daily (24) and weekly (168) cycles may yield multiple strong ACF peaks; which one is optimal may vary.", "Detrending/no-detrending and per-channel vs pooled ACF choices may shift detected peaks; handled as robustness variants.", "Validation-based optimum vs test optimum; and whether the prespecified coverage thresholds are met (to be reported, not claimed)."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9976cacf3aad456db5fe0c36e114fb35", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-9976cacf3aad456db5fe0c36e114fb35", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experiment Design: Using Autocorrelation Analysis to Identify the Optimal Cycle Length for CycleNet in Time Series Forecasting\\n\\n> **Status: design-only. No experiment was run, and no results are reported or fabricated.** All numbers not anchored to the official CycleNet paper/repo are marked **To-Confirm** and are placeholders to be resolved against the public source before execution.\\n\\n## 1. Explicit definitions\\n\\n**D1 \\u2014 Autocorrelation (ACF).** For a training-series segment x of length T, detrended/standardized to mean 0, define the sample autocorrelation at lag h (1\\u2264h\\u2264L): r(h) = \\u03a3_{t=1}^{T\\u2212h} x_t\\u00b7x_{t+h} / \\u03a3_{t=1}^{T} x_t\\u00b2. Candidate periods are lags h where r(h) is a **local maximum** (r(h)>r(h\\u22121) and r(h)>r(h+1)), **above a threshold** c = max(0.3, 2/\\u221aT), with a **minimum separation** \\u0394 between consecutive accepted peaks (\\u0394\\u22485 lags). Candidates are ranked by r(h); the **top-k** (k\\u22485) lags are the ACF-selected candidate cycle lengths. **FFT periodogram** (argmax magnitude \\u2192 period f = T/argmaxbin) is used as an independent cross-check of the top ACF period.\\n\\n**D2 \\u2014 \\\"Optimal cycle length\\\".** For a fixed backbone and fixed hyperparameters, the optimal cycle_len is the value minimizing **mean validation MSE** across seeds; reported **per (dataset, pred_len)** because optimality is assumed horizon-dependent. Test MSE is reported only as a confirmatory check on the validation-selected choice (test set never used for selection \\u2014 no data snooping).\\n\\n**D3 \\u2014 CycleNet cycle_len.** A single positive integer (period in time steps) controlling the Residual Cycle Forecasting Module (RCM), combined with a cycle-aware time-index embedding, per the official ACAT-SCUT/CycleNet design. Changing cycle_len re-parameterizes the time-index to the given periodicity; it does not change the backbone.\\n\\n## 2. Rationale\\n\\nCycleNet requires an externally supplied cycle length; the official workflow hard-codes period values per dataset. Autocorrelation is a natural, data-driven estimator of the dominant period. The research question is whether the ACF-dominant period coincides with \\u2014 and can therefore predict \\u2014 the cycle_len that minimizes predictive error. The experiment operationalizes this as: compute ACF candidates on the training split \\u2192 sweep CycleNet over ACF candidates plus controls \\u2192 measure how well ACF-predicted lengths recover the empirically optimal length and how much accuracy is lost if an off-peak length is chosen.\\n\\n## 3. Materials (To-Confirm against the official repo/paper)\\n\\n- **CycleNet implementation:** official ACAT-SCUT/CycleNet (RCM + cycle-aware time indices). Backbone to fix: **DLinear** as primary; **PatchTST** as a robustness extension (To-Confirm available in the official repo). Default backbone and per-dataset defaults (cycle_len, time_enc_dim, seq_len, pred_len, lr, epochs, seed) are read from the official run scripts and recorded in a setup table before execution. Any value not present in the official scripts is marked To-Confirm.\\n- **Datasets (public knowledge, To-Confirm exact sampling rates/periods):** ETT (h1/h2 hourly, m1/m2 15-min), Electricity (hourly), Traffic (hourly), Weather (10-min per original paper \\u2014 confirm), PEMS-series (5-min), Solar-Energy (10-min), optionally METR-LA / PEMS-BAY (5-min). Known natural periods (ground truth): daily = 24 steps (hourly), 96 (15-min), 144 (10-min), 288 (5-min); weekly = 168 (hourly). Exact availability in the official data scripts is To-Confirm.\\n- **Compute:** provisioned environment with GPU for full runs; CPU-only fallback budget (fewer horizons/epochs) documented in feasibility. This sandbox (no network, no installed numpy/scipy/pandas, no datasets, no GPU) cannot execute it.\\n\\n## 4. Experimental procedure\\n\\n**Stage A \\u2014 ACF period detection (input side).**\\n1. Load each dataset's training split (official 7:1:2 split).\\n2. Standardize per-channel (z-score); optionally detrend (or first-difference) and report both variants; handle multi-channel series by averaging the ACF across channels or by per-channel detection with majority pooling (decide once, record).\\n3. Compute ACF r(h) for h = 1..L, L \\u2248 min(T/3, 3000).\\n4. Peak detection per D1 \\u2192 ranked candidate list C_ACF (top-k \\u22485), plus top FFT period.\\n5. Record the full ACF curve for overlay.\\n\\n**Stage B \\u2014 CycleNet cycle_len sweep (model side).**\\n1. Fix all hyperparameters except cycle_len; fix train/val/test splits and seeds (\\u22653).\\n2. For each dataset \\u00d7 pred_len (e.g., 96/192/336/720 \\u2014 To-Confirm official set), sweep the cycle_len set S:\\n   - positive controls: known true periods of that dataset (e.g., 24, 168 for hourly; scaled for sub-hourly);\\n   - ACF candidates: C_ACF;\\n   - negative/off-peak controls: non-peak lengths around the true period (e.g., true\\u00d7{0.5, 0.75, 1.25, 1.5, 2.0} and true\\u00b110%, true\\u00b125%);\\n   - default baseline: the official per-dataset default cycle_len.\\n   Expected |S| \\u2248 10\\u201315 values per (dataset, pred_len).\\n3. Train each config with \\u22653 seeds; record mean\\u00b1std validation MSE/MAE and test MSE/MAE (test untouched during selection).\\n\\n**Stage C \\u2014 Linking ACF to optimal length.**\\n1. Build per-(dataset, pred_len) curves: mean validation MSE vs cycle_len, with C_ACF candidates and true periods overlaid on the same axis.\\n2. Choose c*_opt = argmin mean val MSE per (dataset, pred_len); confirm with test MSE.\\n\\n## 5. Controls\\n\\n- **Positive controls:** known true periods (24/168 hourly; scaled for 15-min/10-min/5-min) \\u2014 check the sweep's optimum recovers ground truth.\\n- **Negative/off-peak controls:** lengths deliberately away from any ACF peak and away from the true period \\u2014 expected to yield worse (or not better) validation MSE; evidence the surface is meaningful.\\n- **Baseline:** official per-dataset default cycle_len with identical hyperparameters \\u2014 anchors results to the published protocol and quantifies the cost/benefit of ACF-based selection vs the hand-set default.\\n- **Fixed-hyperparameter control:** every configuration shares the same seq_len/dims/lr/epochs/split/seed scheme so only cycle_len varies.\\n- **Seed control:** \\u22653 seeds per config; conclusions drawn only on means consistent across seeds.\\n\\n## 6. Analysis plan (metrics and outputs)\\n\\n- **Primary metric:** validation MSE (means across seeds); secondary: MAE; confirmatory: test MSE at the validation-selected length.\\n- **Outputs:** (i) per-dataset ACF curve with detected peaks vs the sweep's MSE-vs-cycle_len curve on one figure; (ii) per-(dataset, pred_len) table: C_ACF, c*_opt, official default, and MSE at each.\\n- **Linkage metric L1 (ACF-optimum agreement):** does c*_opt fall within the ACF top-k candidate set? Report **coverage@k** = fraction of (dataset, pred_len) cells where the empirical optimum is in the ACF top-k (retrieval-style evaluation across all cells).\\n- **Linkage metric L2 (cycle-length gap):** gap = |c*_ACF \\u2212 c*_opt| for the top ACF candidate c*_ACF (top-1), in steps and as % of c*_opt.\\n- **Linkage metric L3 (surface sharpness/sensitivity):** normalized loss lift at the ACF-selected length, lift(c*_ACF) = (MSE(c*_ACF) \\u2212 MSE(c*_opt)) / MSE(c*_opt); also the half-width of cycle_len values within 1% of the optimum (measures how tolerant selection is).\\n- **Decision rule (primary claim):** autocorrelation identifies the optimal cycle length if coverage@1 \\u2265 0.6 of cells (i.e., top-1 ACF period recovers the empirical optimum in \\u226560% of (dataset, pred_len) cells) and median lift(c*_ACF) \\u2264 1%; coverage@5 \\u2265 0.8 as a secondary robustness claim. These thresholds are prespecified.\\n- **Robustness:** repeat coverage on test MSE (confirmatory), with the detrended-ACF variant, with per-channel vs pooled ACF, and with PatchTST backbone for a subset of cells.\\n\\n## 7. Feasibility\\n\\n- The design is **portable and execution-ready**: it requires only the official CycleNet repo, the public datasets, and a GPU or CPU environment with a working torch stack \\u2014 none present in this sandbox (no network, no deps, no data, no GPU), and this task is design-only.\\n- **GPU path:** ~6\\u20138 datasets \\u00d7 4 pred_len \\u00d7 ~12 cycle_len \\u00d7 3 seeds \\u2248 900\\u20131200 training runs; DLinear runs are minutes each on GPU \\u2192 feasible.\\n- **CPU-only fallback** (if ever executed here, which is not required): reduce to 2 datasets \\u00d7 2 pred_len \\u00d7 ~8 cycle_len \\u00d7 3 seeds and limit epochs; still valid for the linkage analysis though lower-powered. Budget noted as a caveat, not a design change.\\n\\n## 8. Assumptions, uncertainties, and provenance\\n\\n**Assumptions (fixed requirements are marked):** (A1) optimal = min validation MSE per (dataset, pred_len) (fixed requirement). (A2) ACF computed on standardized/detrended training split with threshold+separation peak detection (requirement). (A3) single dominant cycle per run for multi-periodic series (requirement). (A4) only cycle_len varies; all else fixed (requirement). Remaining assumptions are To-Confirm: exact official per-dataset cycle_len/time_enc_dim/backbone/hyperparameters; dataset sampling-rate map; official pred_len set; official split scheme.\\n\\n**Uncertainties to be resolved at execution:** ACF peak may not coincide with predictive optimum at long horizons (exactly what is measured); multi-periodic dominance (daily vs weekly) ambiguity; whether validation-based optimum differs materially from test optimum; effect of the detrending choice on peaks; channel-pooling choice for multivariate ACF.\\n\\n**Provenance:** the only source-supported material in this workspace is task.json stating this is a design-only task; all CycleNet/dataset s